  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-7-PPR-260001027</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-7" type="10">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the Netherlands</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Integrated Strategy for Civic Space and Democratic Resilience in Thailand</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project responds to the need to defend affected individuals and to preserve public memory, expanding public understanding, and strengthens resistance to authoritarianism resulted from the political coup of National Council for Peace and Order (NCPO) era, through an integrated approach combining documentation and archival work, public awareness-raising, knowledge production, legal capacity building, and domestic advocacy.

It seeks to make information on political prosecutions and rights violations more accessible to the public, including through improved digital platforms and creative public engagement. It also aims to create space for academics to contribute research and analysis on political prosecution in Thailand, strengthen the capacity of human rights lawyers to pursue strategic and freedom of expression litigation, and support advocacy for legal and policy reforms.

In doing so, the project aims not only to respond to ongoing repression, but also to help build the social, intellectual, and legal foundations needed to protect civic space and democratic freedoms in Thailand over the longer term through four main objectives:

? Objective 1: Preserve and expand public access to information and knowledge on political repression, political prosecutions, and democratic backsliding in Thailand through archival work, public communication, documentation, and academic engagement.

? Objective 2: Strengthen the capacity, networks, and operational effectiveness of TLHR lawyers and other human rights lawyers to pursue strategic litigation and effectively litigate freedom of expression cases.

? Objective 3: Support domestic advocacy and public engagement for legal and policy reforms that protect fundamental freedoms and reduce the use of law as a tool of political repression.

? Objective 4: Strengthen TLHR&#x2019;s organizational sustainability, internal coordination, and staff capacity to support effective and resilient human rights work in a rapidly changing environment.</narrative>
  </description>
